CONVERSATION:
Was there supposed to be a joint XHTML/SVG/WAI meeting today?
tina: i'll try and ping RichS on that -- he was supposed to organize, and i was supposed to participate, but haven't heard anything (although having said that, i should recheck my email)
50fa2422c663a31e707bdd4f9e4ed4c5: I can't find any mention of such a meeting being called.
tina: there is a PFWG meeting at noon NYC time
Is this a f2f meeting?
tina: it has been discussed in both PF and ARIA subteam meetings
Because the details of the meeting being posted on the xhtml mailing list is an irc one.
i think the original plan was to meet virtually and, depending upon who will be at TPAC, meet there -- let me check if RichS is in the #pf channel
nope -- i'm the only one in #pf right now
Would you happen to know the exact date the call for that meeting was posted?
as far as i know it WASN'T posted -- i was supposed to be in on the call, but it appears that it was held on the sly...
Ok. So if I get this correctly ... a joint XHTML, SVG and ARIA meeting was meant to be held virtually, but the XHTML group members were not informed ... ?
i'm confused, as i had been the one to push for a meeting to get rid of the aria- hack so that ARIA works today and tomorrow -- especially in XML-based languages -- that was 2 weeks ago
not only were XHTML WG members not told, neither were PF members -- including those specifically designated to participate
Very interesting.
very, um, irritating
the HTML players are all attempting to force a decision ASAP -- as is FF
I am tempted to use other words and phrases, but I shall refrain from doing so.
i admire your restraint (grin)
i'll push RichS on the issue at the noon PF call
i'm wary of too much horse-trading being conducted behind closed doors
will calmly ask for a reference to the meeting call.
ok, you're the &quot;good cop&quot; and i'm the &quot;bad cop&quot; -- that's how it usually shakes out in these situations!
http://www.w3.org/2007/10/17-aria-minutes.html
nods, &quot;Just reading those ... &quot;
not a very open attitude from the HTML dev reps, but that's become par for the course...
plans on some &quot;discrete&quot; inquiries vis a vis the meeting without announcement
notes AaronL's statement: Aaron: with a - you can can trigger dynamic updates but without (:) you can
... you have to work around IE problems when you develop cross-browser script
... you don't get anything automatically and people describe what they are doing.
... aria does not drive the behavior
notes comments such as &quot; the people who are implementing svg tiny would be a problem.&quot;
jon gunderson of the university of illinois is working on some IE ARIA demos, as is charles chen, dev of FireVox
notes that its too bad &quot;the people&quot; weren't informed of the meeting being held in their names...
Ok. This, to me, is a problem in itself.
me too...
I am certainly not going to back any sort of decision made in a meeting of that kind.
so are remarks such as: &lt;hsivonen&gt; (I don't really believe in Web language versioning
i'll make an inquiry on the PF list about the meeting and innocently raise the issue of not pre-publicizing the meeting so that there could be broader representation
tina: do you have member access?
50fa2422c663a31e707bdd4f9e4ed4c5: to W3C internals? Yes.
the only thing i could find in the PF archives is an after-the-fact post: http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Member/w3c-wai-pf/2007OctDec/0052.html
i'm going to reply to the reply from the chair AlG on list
there was no one in the XHTML2 WG that had been informed (save, apparently, for StevenP, but he's unavailable for meetings this week, right?)
From a methodology point of view, this is unfortunate. The agreements made during that meeting cannot be considered valid for the XHTML WG. From what I could see, Steven was supposed to be there. He was ALSO supposed to chair a meeting here today.
yeah...
